('mock-eng-boathouse-q05', 'english', 'QT-RC-04', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 150,
 $json${"id":"mock-eng-boathouse-q05","marks":4,"skill":"vocabulary","question":"Write a synonym (a word or short phrase with the same meaning) for each of the following words as used in the passage: (a) 'convinced' -- persuaded (this one is done for you as an example); (b) 'faintly'; (c) 'steadier'; (d) 'wedged'; (e) 'rush' (in 'don't rush the varnish').","modelAnswer":"(b) faintly: slightly. (c) steadier: calmer/more settled/more secure. (d) wedged: jammed/squeezed/stuck. (e) rush: hurry/hasten.","passageTitle":"The Boat in the Boathouse","passageText":"Two summers ago, their grandfather had promised Priya and her brother Kabir that once the old rowing boat was fit to float again, the first trip would be theirs alone, no adults, all the way to Heron Island and back. He had died before the boat was finished, and for two years afterwards nobody in the family had gone near the boathouse at all.\n\nIt was Kabir who suggested, on a wet Saturday in April, that they finish it themselves.\n\nPriya was not convinced. The boat, when they finally dragged the tarpaulin off it, looked less like a promise and more like a problem: the hull was grey with dust, one oar was missing its blade entirely, and a long crack ran along the bottom board like a fault line. \"This isn't finishing it,\" she said. \"This is starting again from nothing.\"\n\n\"Then we start again from nothing,\" Kabir said, and began, with a patience she had never seen in him before, to sort the tools their grandfather had left hanging on the boathouse wall into three careful piles: the ones that still worked, the ones that needed sharpening, and the ones that were simply too rusted to save.\n\nPriya watched him work for a long time before she picked up a piece of sandpaper herself.\n\nOver the following month they worked most weekends, and the boat changed slowly, in a way that made it hard, afterwards, to say exactly when it had stopped being a wreck and started being a boat again. Kabir turned out to be meticulous in a way that surprised her: he would sand one plank for twenty minutes to get it perfectly smooth, checking it constantly with the flat of his hand, refusing to move on until it satisfied him. Priya worked faster and more impatiently, and twice had to redo a section Kabir quietly pointed out was still rough, without saying anything unkind about it.\n\nIt was Priya who found the note. It had been wedged, folded twice, into a gap behind the boat's rear seat, and the paper had gone soft and faintly yellow with age. She almost threw it away without opening it, assuming it was rubbish left by the boat's long-ago builder. It was their grandfather's handwriting.\n\nFor whoever finds this, patch the crack in the bottom board with the proper marine filler, not the quick stuff, or it'll only last a season. Sand with the grain, always. And don't rush the varnish; three thin coats beat one thick one every time. If it's you two reading this, I'm sorry I couldn't finish it myself. Take her out gently the first time. She'll remember how to float.\n\nPriya read it twice before she could speak. Kabir, when she finally showed him, said nothing at all for almost a minute, just looked at the crack in the bottom board as though seeing it properly for the first time.\n\nThey did everything the note said, in order: first the crack, filled and left to cure for two full days before either of them touched it again; then the sanding, redone more carefully than before, always with the grain this time; and finally the varnish, three thin coats, each one left overnight, though Priya privately thought the third coat looked exactly the same as the second.\n\nThe morning they finally carried the boat down to the water, neither of them said very much. Kabir held it steady while Priya climbed in first, and for a moment, as the boat settled and did not sink and did not leak, she felt something she hadn't expected to feel: not triumph, exactly, but something quieter and steadier than that.\n\n\"Well,\" Kabir said eventually, climbing in after her and picking up an oar. \"She remembered.\"","acceptedAnswers":["slightly","dimly","calmer","more settled","more secure","more composed","jammed","squeezed","stuck","forced","hurry","hasten","do quickly","speed up"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'Mock Programme Increment 006, English Batch 001 (Track B). QT-RC-04 (Synonym Substitution List), competency RC-03, family mock-eng-rc04-synonymlist. Evidence: Confidence MEDIUM, EMC-3 (identical five-item/worked-example format, two of three years). All 5 words independently re-checked as appearing in this migration''s own passage text before authoring; item (a) is the required worked example, per the Framework''s own format description.', 3, 'mock-eng-boathouse',
 'mock-eng-rc04-synonymlist', 'angel_original', 'authentic_assessment_candidate', 1, true,
 'Guessing a synonym from a word''s sound or a loosely related idea rather than the meaning the word actually carries in its specific sentence context.',
 'NEAR_TRANSFER'),

-- === Q6 (QT-RC-05, Quotation-and-Explanation; MEDIUM/EMC-3) ============
('mock-eng-boathouse-q06', 'english', 'QT-RC-05', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 120,
 $json${"id":"mock-eng-boathouse-q06","marks":2,"skill":"evidence","question":"Find a quotation from the passage that shows Kabir's approach to repairing the boat was different from Priya's. Explain what the quotation shows.","modelAnswer":"Kabir 'would sand one plank for twenty minutes to get it perfectly smooth', showing he worked slowly and carefully, checking his work constantly, whereas Priya worked faster and more impatiently and sometimes had to redo rough sections.","passageTitle":"The Boat in the Boathouse","passageText":"Two summers ago, their grandfather had promised Priya and her brother Kabir that once the old rowing boat was fit to float again, the first trip would be theirs alone, no adults, all the way to Heron Island and back. He had died before the boat was finished, and for two years afterwards nobody in the family had gone near the boathouse at all.\n\nIt was Kabir who suggested, on a wet Saturday in April, that they finish it themselves.\n\nPriya was not convinced. The boat, when they finally dragged the tarpaulin off it, looked less like a promise and more like a problem: the hull was grey with dust, one oar was missing its blade entirely, and a long crack ran along the bottom board like a fault line. \"This isn't finishing it,\" she said. \"This is starting again from nothing.\"\n\n\"Then we start again from nothing,\" Kabir said, and began, with a patience she had never seen in him before, to sort the tools their grandfather had left hanging on the boathouse wall into three careful piles: the ones that still worked, the ones that needed sharpening, and the ones that were simply too rusted to save.\n\nPriya watched him work for a long time before she picked up a piece of sandpaper herself.\n\nOver the following month they worked most weekends, and the boat changed slowly, in a way that made it hard, afterwards, to say exactly when it had stopped being a wreck and started being a boat again. Kabir turned out to be meticulous in a way that surprised her: he would sand one plank for twenty minutes to get it perfectly smooth, checking it constantly with the flat of his hand, refusing to move on until it satisfied him. Priya worked faster and more impatiently, and twice had to redo a section Kabir quietly pointed out was still rough, without saying anything unkind about it.\n\nIt was Priya who found the note. It had been wedged, folded twice, into a gap behind the boat's rear seat, and the paper had gone soft and faintly yellow with age. She almost threw it away without opening it, assuming it was rubbish left by the boat's long-ago builder. It was their grandfather's handwriting.\n\nFor whoever finds this, patch the crack in the bottom board with the proper marine filler, not the quick stuff, or it'll only last a season. Sand with the grain, always. And don't rush the varnish; three thin coats beat one thick one every time. If it's you two reading this, I'm sorry I couldn't finish it myself. Take her out gently the first time. She'll remember how to float.\n\nPriya read it twice before she could speak. Kabir, when she finally showed him, said nothing at all for almost a minute, just looked at the crack in the bottom board as though seeing it properly for the first time.\n\nThey did everything the note said, in order: first the crack, filled and left to cure for two full days before either of them touched it again; then the sanding, redone more carefully than before, always with the grain this time; and finally the varnish, three thin coats, each one left overnight, though Priya privately thought the third coat looked exactly the same as the second.\n\nThe morning they finally carried the boat down to the water, neither of them said very much. Kabir held it steady while Priya climbed in first, and for a moment, as the boat settled and did not sink and did not leak, she felt something she hadn't expected to feel: not triumph, exactly, but something quieter and steadier than that.\n\n\"Well,\" Kabir said eventually, climbing in after her and picking up an oar. \"She remembered.\"","quotationRequired":["he would sand one plank for twenty minutes to get it perfectly smooth"],"validationTier":"TIER3_QUOTATION_PLUS_EXPLANATION"}$json$,
 'Mock Programme Increment 006, English Batch 001 (Track B). QT-RC-05 (Quotation-and-Explanation), competency RC-02, family mock-eng-rc05-quoteexplain. Evidence: Confidence MEDIUM, EMC-3 (explicit labelled quotation-then-explanation structure, two of three years). quotationRequired independently re-checked as an exact substring of this migration''s own passage text.', 2, 'mock-eng-boathouse',
 'mock-eng-rc05-quoteexplain', 'angel_original', 'authentic_assessment_candidate', 1, true,
 'Selecting a quotation that describes what a character did in general, rather than one that specifically demonstrates the contrast in approach between the two characters.',
 'MIXED_TRANSFER'),

